          %title The axiom of foundation
          %h1#the_axiom_of_foundation The axiom of foundation
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#statement"} Statement
              %li
                %a{:href => "#alternative_formulations"} Alternative formulations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#antifoundation"} Anti-foundation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#structural_meaning"} Structural meaning
              %li
                %a{:href => "#see_also"} See also
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %p
            In material
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            = succeed "," do
              %strong axiom of foundation
            = succeed "," do
              %strong axiom of regularity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            on the proper class of all
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
          %h2#statement Statement
          %p
            Given a proper class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            has the property that, given any pure set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall t,\; t \in x \Rightarrow t \in A ,
          %p
            then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            may be called a
            %em membership-inductive
            class. Then the
            %strong axiom of foundation
            states that the only membership-inductive class of pure sets is the class of all pure sets. In this form, the axiom of foundation is also called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∈
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
                \-induction
          %p Although the statement here refers to proper classes, it can also be formulated as an axiom schema that makes no mention of classes.
          %h2#alternative_formulations Alternative formulations
          %p
            While the statement above follows how the axiom of foundation is generally
            %em used
            —to prove properties of pure sets by
            = succeed "\u2014," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+induction"} transfinite induction
          #InfiniteDescent.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              The axiom of foundation holds if and only if there exists no infinite descending
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \in x_2 \in x_1 \in x_0
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              First, suppose the axiom of foundation holds, and suppose there were such a sequence. Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              be the class of all sets which are not equal to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              for any
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              were equal to some
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in x
              would also be equal to some
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_j
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_{i+1}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in x
              are in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %p
              Second, suppose there are no such sequences, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              be as in the statement of foundation. Suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              does not contain all sets. Then there exists an
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0\notin A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1\in x_0
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 \notin A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2\in x_1
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2 \notin A
              = succeed "-sequence" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∈
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              must contain all sets.
          %p
            This is essentially a version of Fermat's method of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+descent"} infinite descent
            modified to apply to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+induction"} transfinite induction
            instead of only to ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \in x\in x\in x
            would be an infinite chain.
          #MembershipMinimal.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              The axiom of foundation holds if and only if every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
              pure set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              has a member
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in x
              such that no
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in x
              satisfies
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              is called a
              %em membership-minimal
              element of
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              First, suppose the axiom of foundation holds, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              be a pure set without a membership-minimal element, and let
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \{ y | y\notin x \}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
              is a set such that all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in z
              are in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\in x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in z
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in x
              and hence
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\notin A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\in A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              satisfies the assumptions of the foundation axiom, and hence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is empty.
            %p
              Second, suppose that every inhabited pure set has a membership-minimal element, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              be as in the statement of foundation. Since every pure set is contained in a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+set"} transitive set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cap x = x
              for any transitive
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = \{ z\in x \mid z \notin A \}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              is inhabited, then it contains a membership-minimal element
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\in x
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\notin A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in z
              we have
              = succeed "\u2014but" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\notin z
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in x
              since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is transitive, hence
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
              contradicts the assumption on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              must be empty, as desired.
          %p
            This version is favoured by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical
            set theorists as a statement of the axiom, since it uses neither higher-order reasoning (as our first definition does) or infinity (as infinite descent does).
          %p
            However, neither of these is acceptable in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            to prove at least one direction. In particular, the nonexistence of infinite descending sequences is too weak to allow proofs by transfinite induction (except for special forms of
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p More precisely, the necessary set-theoretic axioms for the above proofs are the following. (Is it known, in any case, that proofs using fewer axioms don’t exist?)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The “only if” direction of Lemma
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InfiniteDescent"}
                requires only the axiom of infinity (for “infinite sequence” to make sense).
            %li
              %p
                The “if” direction of Lemma
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InfiniteDescent"}
                evidently requires the principle of excluded middle, the axiom of infinity, and the axiom of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+collection"} axiom of collection
                (since dependent choice, as usually stated, only chooses elements from a sequence of nonempty
                = succeed "," do
                  %em sets
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
                strong enough to recursively construct functions into a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
                (which is usually proven using the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+separation"} axiom of separation
            %li
              %p
                The “only if” direction of Lemma
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MembershipMinimal"}
                requires only excluded middle.
            %li
              %p
                The “if” direction of Lemma
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MembershipMinimal"}
                requires excluded middle, also that every set is contained in a transitive one (the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+transitive+closure"} axiom of transitive closure
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"} replacement
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+separation"} axiom of separation
                in the form “the intersection of any class with a set is a set.”
          %p
            Another version of the axiom of foundation which
            %em is
            intuitionistically acceptable, but makes no reference to proper classes is:
          #WFTransitive.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_3 Lemma
            %p
              The axiom of foundation holds if and only if the relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
              on any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+set"} transitive set
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded relation
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Suppose the axiom of foundation holds, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              be a transitive set, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\subseteq x
              be such that for any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in y
              are in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              be the class of all sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              such that if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in x
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              satisfies the conditions in the axiom of foundation, so it contains all sets, and hence
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S=x
            %p
              Now suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
              is well-founded on any transitive set and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              satisfy the conditions in foundation. Since every set is contained in a transitive one, it suffices to show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\cap x = x
              for any transitive
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p In this case:
          %ul
            %li
              %p The “only if” direction requires no notable axioms at all, while
            %li
              %p
                The “if” direction requires
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+closure"} transitive closure
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MembershipMinimal"}
          %p
            Finally, another commonly cited version of foundation, equivalent to it at least over the other axioms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZF"} ZF
          #CumulativeHierarchy.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_4 Lemma
            %p
              The axiom of foundation holds if and only if every pure set is an element of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\alpha
              for some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal"} ordinal
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %p
            Here the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\alpha
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cumulative+hierarchy"} cumulative hierarchy
            defined by
            %span.newWikiWord
              transfinite recursion
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/transfinite+recursion"} ?
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\alpha = P(\bigcup_{\beta\lt \alpha} V_\beta)
          %h2#antifoundation Anti-foundation
          %p Most of set theory works without the axiom of foundation, but not the deep study of well-founded pure sets. However, one might want to do material set theory without assuming that all sets are well-founded, then one would not assume this axiom.
          %p
            Alternatively, one can adopt the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong axiom of anti-foundation
          %ul
            %li
              Given any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+relation"} extensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relation"} binary relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
              on any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+set"} transitive set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U,\in)
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              (necessarily uniquely) to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≺
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,\prec)
          %p
            Since any relation has an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+quotient"} extensional quotient
          %ul
            %li
              Given any binary relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
              on any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+set"} transitive set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : S \to U
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(s_1) \in f(s_2)
              if and only if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_1 \in s_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_1, s_2
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is almost an isomorphism between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≺
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S, \prec)
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U, \in)
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injective
          %p
            Just as there are several versions of an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+relation"} extensional relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+extensionality"} axiom of extensionality
            (which is equivalent to the usual one for well-founded sets).
          %p
            If you include the hypothesis that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≺
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mostowski%27s+collapsing+lemma"} Mostowski's collapsing lemma
          %p
            If you adopt the axiom of anti-foundation (with the strongest notion of extensional relation) instead of foundation, then the universe of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+sets"} pure sets
            becomes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/corecursion"} corecursively
            defined ill-founded sets instead of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursively
            defined well-founded sets.
          %h2#structural_meaning Structural meaning
          %p
            Since the axiom of foundation is about pure sets, there seems little point to it in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the underlying set of elements of a well-founded model for a pure set (in any of the ways described at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordering+theorem"} well-ordering theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the underlying set of a model for a von Neumann
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
          %p
            That this statement is the correct structural version of antifoundation may be justified by appeal to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material-structural+adjunction"} material-structural adjunction
          %h2#see_also See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mostowski%27s+principle"} Mostowski's principle
          %h2#references References
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-wellfounded+sets"} non-wellfounded sets
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%A5kon+Robbestad+Gylterud"}> Håkon Robbestad Gylterud
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elisabeth+Stenholm"} Elisabeth Stenholm
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niccol%C3%B2+Veltri"} Niccolò Veltri
              %em Terminal Coalgebras and Non-wellfounded Sets in Homotopy Type Theory
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.06696"}> arXiv:2001.06696
              \&amp;rbrack;
          .property
            category:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/foundational+axiom"} foundational axiom
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/logic"} logic
